before:
```typescript
interface NamedItem { title: string }
interface TimedItem extends NamedItem { minutes: number }
function timedLabel(item: TimedItem): string {
  return item.title + ": " + item.minutes;
}
console.log(timedLabel({title:"Sketch", minutes:12}));
```

This prints Sketch: 12. timedLabel receives an object containing title and minutes, reads both values, and returns display text without changing the object. The interface declarations do not create the object; the literal inside the call does.

after:
TimedItem needs both the shared title and its own minutes value.
```typescript
interface NamedItem { title: string; }
interface TimedItem extends NamedItem { minutes: number; }
function timedLabel(item: TimedItem): string {
  return item.title + ": " + item.minutes;
}
console.log(timedLabel({ title: "Sketch", minutes: 12 }));
```
The output is Sketch: 12.
